[Your Reasoning]: I used all 24 measurements as bounce trials, so each release supplied two constraints: first-impact time and post-impact apex height.

The release heights were spread densely from 0.05 to 2.50 m, with extra sampling around changes in the impact-time trend. The low-height trials show appreciable drag immediately above the floor, followed by a pronounced reduction near 0.2–0.3 m. The progressively larger time delays and declining rebound-height fraction indicate increasing drag through roughly 0.5–1.6 m, including a strong region around 0.8–1.6 m. The unusually small impact-time increments above about 1.6 m indicate a sharp reduction there; smaller upper-height variations suggest a secondary bump near 1.9–2.1 m and weaker drag around 2.2 m.

I accounted for the 0.03 m radius offset when expressing the submitted profile in centre height. I also used the rebound data to constrain the near-floor portion traversed both downward and upward. The submitted samples form a smooth, nonnegative approximation rather than presumed discrete layers. Above the measured centre-height range, I extended the final trend conservatively because the experiments do not directly identify that region.
